# stackcone Solutions posts

When the user pastes a **problem statement** or asks for a **solution post**, produce a full **proposed solution** brief in `solutions/` — same visual system as `blog/` (charts, Mermaid diagrams, tables, SEO). The page should read like a technical proposal and implementation plan: how to architect, build, operate, and report. **Do not** frame it as work already delivered or cite Upwork (or any job board) as the source.

## Deliverables (always)

1. `solutions/posts/{slug}.html` — published page
2. `solutions/posts/{slug}.md` — markdown source (mirror of content)
3. Update `solutions/solutions.json` — add entry with `"author": "Amar Kumar"`
4. Update `sitemap.xml` — add `<url>` for the new HTML page

**Slug:** lowercase, hyphenated (e.g. `wordpress-seo-multi-agent-pipeline`). Files live in `solutions/posts/{slug}.html` and `solutions/posts/{slug}.md`.

## solutions.json entry

```json
{
  "id": "{slug}",
  "title": "Outcome-focused title",
  "description": "Problem + approach in one sentence (under ~155 chars)",
  "href": "./posts/{slug}.html",
  "date": "YYYY-MM-DD",
  "dateLabel": "Month YYYY",
  "author": "Amar Kumar",
  "category": "RAG | Data | API | Mobile | Docs | Platform | Automation",
  "tags": ["3–6 tags"],
  "client": "Company name if mentioned — omit otherwise"
}
```

Omit `client` if no company named. **Do not** add `source` fields (no `upwork`, no job-board provenance).

## Article structure (required)

Use `blog-page` body, `blog-article`, `../styles.css` + `../blog/blog.css` + `solutions.css`, `/site-nav.js`, `/script.js`.

| Section | ID | Content |
|---------|-----|---------|
| Title + meta | — | H1; `<p class="meta">By Amar Kumar</p>` (no date on page) |
| Summary | — | 2–3 sentences: **proposed** outcome and approach (future tense / “would”) |
| Table of contents | `.toc` | All major sections |
| Scenario | `#scenario` | Use case pattern, platform, owner profile, scope — state this is a **proposed solution** |
| Problem | `#problem` | Pain points, why manual flow fails, YMYL/regulatory notes if relevant |
| Requirements | `#requirements` | Functional + non-functional bullets |
| Architecture | `#architecture` | **Mermaid.js** diagrams (not ASCII): system flowchart + sequence/UML view + optional LR pipeline; **Chart.js** component/layer chart in `.chart-wrap--architecture` |
| Recommended stack | `#recommended-stack` | Table + **why this stack** (vs n8n-only, vs alternatives) |
| Agent / component design | `#agent-design` | Per-agent responsibilities, inputs/outputs, QA gates |
| Implementation plan | `#implementation` | `.impl-phase` blocks — **4–6 phases** with deliverables, risks, rollback |
| Reporting & ops | `#reporting` | Dashboards, APIs (GSC, Bing, rank tracking), alert cadence |
| Proposed deliverables | `#deliverables` | 5+ concrete artifacts the build **would** ship (not “what we built”) |
| Effort estimate | `#estimate` | Hours range, ongoing maintenance — indicative, no job-post pricing |
| Charts | — | **At least 3** Chart.js charts in `.chart-wrap` (include one architecture/component map chart) |
| Glossary | `#glossary` | Terms table |
| CTA | `.blog-cta` | Contact primary; Upwork link optional secondary |

## Tone and framing

- **Proposed solution / implementation plan** — not a case study or portfolio win
- Use: “proposed”, “would include”, “following this plan”, “indicative effort”
- Avoid: “we built”, “what we delivered”, “source: Upwork”, “client hired us”, past-tense delivery claims
- Do **not** fabricate company names or fake traffic numbers; describe credible architecture and measurable KPIs instead
- Upwork job posts may be **input** for requirements — never **output** as labeled source on the page

Also add JSON-LD `BreadcrumbList`: Home → Solutions → page title (canonical URL on each item).

## When a **company name** is mentioned

Set `client`, name in Problem or Scenario, full component diagram, 4–6 implementation phases with timelines, ops runbooks, extra chart (deployment or request lifecycle).

## HTML conventions

- **Architecture diagrams:** Mermaid.js CDN (`mermaid@10`); use `<div class="mermaid">` (not `<pre>`). Wrap in `.diagram-wrap--fit` for vertical flowcharts (`useMaxWidth: true`) or `.diagram-wrap--scroll` for wide sequence diagrams (`useMaxWidth: false`, horizontal scroll). Use `.pipeline` for simple LR step flows. Run `mermaid.run()` on `window.load`. Light theme with `mainBkg: '#ffffff'`.
- Simple flows can use Mermaid `flowchart LR` instead of `.pipeline` on solution pages (blog pages may still use `.pipeline`).
- Charts: Chart.js CDN; palette `['#2563eb', '#7c3aed', '#db2777', '#ea580c', '#16a34a', '#0891b2']`
- Initialize Mermaid before Chart.js; `mermaid.initialize({ startOnLoad: true, securityLevel: 'loose' })`
- Visible author: **By Amar Kumar**

## SEO — meta tags and keywords (required)

Follow `.cursor/rules/seo-google.mdc`. Every new solution page must have **unique, keyword-targeted** head tags — not copy-pasted from other solutions.

### Keyword research (before writing `<head>`)

1. Pick **one primary keyword** (what a buyer would search, e.g. `multi-agent SEO pipeline`, `WordPress AI content automation`).
2. Pick **3–5 secondary keywords** (stack, niche, outcome, e.g. `LangGraph`, `Search Console API`, `YMYL content`).
3. Use primary keyword in **title**, **H1** (natural phrasing), first summary paragraph, and **meta description**.
4. Scatter secondary keywords in H2s, stack table, tags, and body — never stuff.

### Required `<head>` fields

| Tag | Rules |
|-----|--------|
| `<title>` | Primary keyword + outcome; `\| stackcone Solutions` suffix; **≤ 60 chars** |
| `<meta name="description">` | Value prop + primary keyword + 1 secondary; **≤ 155 chars**; unique per page |
| `<meta name="keywords">` | **6–10** comma-separated terms: primary, secondaries, niche, stack names |
| `<link rel="canonical">` | `https://stackcone.com/solutions/posts/{slug}.html` — exact URL |
| `og:title` | Same as title or slightly shorter (no suffix if too long) |
| `og:description` | Can differ from meta description but same intent; **≤ 155 chars** |
| `og:url` | Same as canonical |
| `og:type` | `article` |
| `og:image` | `https://stackcone.com/logo/stackcone.png` |
| `twitter:card` | `summary_large_image` |
| `twitter:title` / `twitter:description` | Align with OG |

### JSON-LD `Article` block

Include in every solution HTML:

- `headline` — matches H1
- `description` — matches or expands meta description
- `keywords` — **array of 6–10 strings** (mirror `meta keywords` plus 1–2 long-tail phrases)
- `articleSection` — match `category` from `solutions.json` (e.g. `Automation`, `RAG`)
- `datePublished` / `dateModified` — post date
- `author` / `publisher` — as in existing blog/solution templates
- `mainEntityOfPage.@id` — canonical URL

### `solutions.json` alignment

Keep listing metadata aligned with SEO (helps internal search + consistency):

- `title` — same as H1 (short form OK)
- `description` — **same text as** `<meta name="description">` (or trim to ~155 chars)
- `tags` — overlap with `meta keywords` (3–6 tags)

### On-page SEO

- **One H1** only; must include primary keyword naturally
- Section H2s use secondary keywords where accurate (`#architecture`, `#recommended-stack`, etc.)
- TOC links use descriptive anchor IDs (already in template)
- Chart `aria-label` and captions describe the chart in plain language (indexable adjacent text)
- CTA links: descriptive text (`Contact us`, not `click here`)

### Avoid

- Duplicate title/description across solutions
- Keyword stuffing in meta tags or headings
- Missing canonical or wrong domain/path
- Empty `keywords` or JSON-LD `keywords` array
- Upwork / job-board **source** labels on the page or in JSON


## Quality bar

- Teach how to build the system, not just what it does
- Problem → architecture → stack → phases → reporting must be one coherent story
- No lorem ipsum; no thin bullet lists without explanation
